<script>
import { type RouteLiteral, url } from '@abide/abide'

import Doc from '#src/lib/Doc.abide'
import Lead from '#src/lib/Lead.abide'
import Section from '#src/lib/Section.abide'
import { SITE } from '#src/lib/SITE.ts'
import { pageAt } from '#src/lib/sitePages.ts'

// The chapters, as `SITE` lists them (40.31).
const chapters = SITE.flatMap(({ pages }) =>
    pages.filter((page) => page.route.startsWith('/guide/')),
)
const reference = pageAt('/reference')
</script>

<Doc>
    <p>In abide a value on the server is something a page reads, and reading it loads it. The
        handler you would have written anyway is the whole of the API, its type is the same on both
        sides, and loading, reloading, streaming and failing are all one type.</p>

    <p>These docs build one app, a CRM, in ten chapters. Each chapter adds what the CRM needs next,
        and ends with the CRM as an app you can download and run. Every card is a file from that app,
        with the tests and the bench the app runs on it.</p>

    <p>The handler a page calls is also an OpenAPI operation, an MCP tool and a CLI command: one
        handler, four surfaces, so a model reaches the CRM through the same code a person does. See
        <a href={url('/guide/open-it-to-machines')}>{pageAt('/guide/open-it-to-machines').nav}</a>.</p>

    <Section title="The guide, chapter by chapter">
        <ol>
            {#for chapter of chapters by chapter.route}
                <li>
                    <p><a href={url(chapter.route as RouteLiteral)}>{chapter.nav}</a></p>
                    <Lead of={chapter.route} />
                </li>
            {/for}
        </ol>
    </Section>

    <Section title={reference.title}>
        <Lead of="/reference" />
        <p>Read on: <a href={url(reference.route as RouteLiteral)}>{reference.title}</a></p>
    </Section>
</Doc>
